{
  "name": "How to Prioritize Accessible Home Modifications",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Making your home more accessible can feel like a big project, so knowing where to start is key. A great way to approach it is by following a structured process. First, you'll want to conduct a comprehensive needs assessment. This means thinking about your current mobility and how it might evolve in the future. Don't just focus on today; consider what you might need a few years down the line. Next, systematically review your entire home. Pay close attention to entryways, ensuring they're easy to navigate, and check if your doors offer sufficient width. Evaluate your flooring for potential tripping hazards and ensure good lighting throughout. Don't forget about light switches and other controls; are they easy to reach and operate? Lastly, assess existing safety devices. Once you've identified all the potential barriers, you can prioritize modifications like adding ramps, widening doorways, improving lighting, installing grab bars, switching to non-slip flooring, or even considering vertical-access solutions for stairs. If you're unsure about the best approach, especially for larger structural changes, reaching out to an experienced professional can make a huge difference in ensuring your home truly supports your needs.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
